    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (73.77) and **Yordan Alvarez** (72.97) leading the charge as our premier offensive engines. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** remains our most critical asset in the scarcity-driven SB category; facing Gausman, his dynamic platoon advantage and a high-efficiency environment against a pitcher with a slow delivery make him an elite play. Simultaneously, **Yordan Alvarez** remains a lock in the utility spot; despite facing Bibee, his elite 0.450 xwOBA provides the floor necessary to anchor our lineup regardless of the opposing SP_xERA. **Junior Caminero** (67.50) also earns a high-confidence start, as he is perfectly positioned to exploit the 5.00 xERA of Poulin, with the +19% SP skill boost overriding his moderate platoon concerns.\n\nThe optimization process faced a tight margin between **Christian Yelich** (59.62) and our bench options, with **Yelich** narrowly retaining the final outfield spot despite a projected drop due to the L/L matchup against P\u00e9rez. The decision to bench potential depth pieces was largely driven by our strict adherence to the 40-point efficiency floor. While we are monitoring the status of players like **Carson Benge** and **Gary Sanchez** for future utility, their profiles currently lack the specific SB upside or platoon efficiency required to displace **Lars Nootbaar** (61.48) or **Casey Schmitt** (42.28) today. We have confirmed that **Hyeseong Kim** and **Rece Hinds** remain in the minors, and with **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber** sidelined by team off-days, our bench remains thin but focused on preserving game caps for high-leverage spots. \n\nWe are keeping a close watch on **Dillon Dingler** (54.77), whose value is significantly buoyed by an order-two promotion and favorable platoon metrics against Eisert. However, he remains a marginal start; if the wind conditions at the park shift against the current projections, we may see his efficiency dip closer to the danger zone. **Chase Meidroth** (43.09) is also at risk facing Skubal, an elite arm with a 2.91 xERA. While his batting order slot provides a cushion, we are prepared to evaluate his performance early as we navigate a day where pitching matchups dictate a more conservative approach to our active roster.",
